# SCSS Reference

## Critical

```scss
// ❌ NEVER:
font-size: 18px; // raw px
@media (min-width: 768px) {
} // raw px in media query
@media (width < $md) {
} // desktop-first — NOT allowed in this project
@include bp-down() {
} // legacy helper
@include bp-up() {
} // legacy helper
font-size: 18px;
@media (width >= $md) {
	font-size: 22px;
} // don't use @media to change values — use fluid()

// ✅ ALWAYS:
gap: rem-calc(16); // Fixed values → rem-calc()
font-size: fluid(16, 24); // Responsive values → fluid(min, max)
@media (width >= $md) {
} // Mobile-first only — progressive enhancement for layout changes
```

## Media Query Rule (READ THIS)

This project uses two complementary tools for responsiveness:

1. **`fluid(min, max)` for _values_** — font sizes, spacing, padding, margins, gaps, radii. Never change these with `@media`.
2. **`@media` for _layout changes only_** — grid column count, flex direction, show/hide, position. Allowed when a layout genuinely restructures at a breakpoint, not as a way to tweak values.

**All `@media` queries MUST be mobile-first** (`width >= $bp`), not desktop-first (`width < $bp`). Write the mobile layout as the default, then progressively enhance for larger screens.

```scss
// ✅ GOOD — fluid() for values, mobile-first @media only for layout shape
.card-grid {
	display: grid;
	grid-template-columns: 1fr; // Mobile default
	gap: fluid(16, 32); // Value scales fluidly — no @media needed

	@media (width >= $md) {
		grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); // Layout change
	}

	@media (width >= $lg) {
		grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); // Layout change
	}
}

// ❌ BAD — desktop-first + @media used to change a value
.card-grid {
	grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); // desktop default
	gap: rem-calc(32);

	@media (width < $md) {
		// desktop-first direction forbidden
		grid-template-columns: 1fr;
		gap: rem-calc(16); // value change — should be fluid() instead
	}
}
```

**If the only thing changing at a breakpoint is a value (font-size, padding, gap, etc.), use `fluid()`, not `@media`.**

## Functions

```scss
rem-calc(16)              // → 1rem
fluid(16, 24)             // Responsive: 16px min, 24px max
fluid(16, 24, md, xl)     // Custom breakpoints
```

### `fluid()` mechanics

`fluid($min, $max, $min-bp: 'md', $max-bp: 'xl')` compiles to a `clamp()` expression. Below `$min-bp` the value stays at `$min`; above `$max-bp` it stays at `$max`; between the two it scales linearly. Default span is `$md` (768px) → `$xl` (1200px). Don't widen the third / fourth arguments without an explicit reason — wider spans flatten the curve and the value barely moves through the middle viewports where most users actually sit.

### Sass interpolation in CSS `min()` / `max()`

`#{…}` interpolation is required when a Sass variable appears inside CSS `min()` or `max()` — without it Sass treats them as its own functions and refuses to compile.

```scss
// ❌ NEVER:
max-inline-size: min($container-max, 100%);

// ✅ ALWAYS:
max-inline-size: min(#{$container-max}, 100%);
```

## Breakpoints

<!-- @project-config:breakpoint-tokens -->
```scss
$xs: 0px;
$ph: 375px;
$sm: 576px;
$md: 768px;
$lg: 1024px;
$xl: 1200px;
$xxl: 1400px;
$xxxl: 1600px;
```
<!-- @end -->

**No custom breakpoints.** You cannot add breakpoints of your own. Every `@media` query must use one of the breakpoints defined in `src/sass/partials/config/_breakpoints.scss` (`$xs`, `$ph`, `$sm`, `$md`, `$lg`, `$xl`, `$xxl`, `$xxxl`), and must reference the **variable name** — never the raw px value.

```scss
// ❌ NEVER:
@media (width >= 900px) {
} // raw px
@media (width >= 820px) {
} // unlisted breakpoint

// ✅ ALWAYS:
@media (width >= $md) {
} // use the variable from _breakpoints.scss
```

## Responsive Design

**Mobile-first approach** — write mobile styles as the default, then progressively enhance for larger screens using `@media (width >= $bp)`. Desktop-first `@media (width < $bp)` is **not allowed** in this project.

```scss
.component {
	// Mobile styles (default, unconditional)
	display: block;
	grid-template-columns: 1fr;
	gap: fluid(16, 24); // Value scales fluidly — no @media needed

	// Tablet+ — layout change only
	@media (width >= $md) {
		display: flex;
		grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
	}

	// Desktop+ — layout change only
	@media (width >= $lg) {
		grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr);
	}
}
```

**Remember:** `@media` is for _layout changes_ (grid columns, flex direction, visibility), not _value changes_. Use `fluid()` for values that scale.

<!-- @project-config:legacy-partials -->
**Legacy exception:** A few pre-existing partials (`src/sass/partials/components/_header-menu.scss`, `src/sass/partials/mixins/_miscellaneous.scss` — the `swiper-cards-overflow-down` mixin) still use desktop-first `@media (width < $bp)` because rewriting them carries regression risk on live, shared UI. Mobile-first is **mandatory for all new code**, all block SCSS, and any partial you touch for unrelated reasons. Don't copy the legacy pattern into new work.
<!-- @end -->

## Exceptions

1px borders, box shadows, values under 4px

## Config tokens — reuse first

Before writing any rule using a color, font, font-size, spacing, breakpoint, z-index, or easing value, check the config partials and reuse what already exists. If a new shared token is needed, add it to the matching config file rather than hardcoding inline.

<!-- @project-config:typography-tokens -->
**`src/sass/partials/config/_typography.scss`**

- Vars: `$serif`, `$sans-serif`, `$body-font-size`, `$body-font-family`, `$heading-font-family`, `$paragraph-line-height`, `$paragraph-margin-block-end`, `$heading-margin-block-end`, `$headings`
- Map: `$typography-styles` (h1–h6, display-1 / display-2 / display-3)
- Mixins: `@include font(h1..h6 | display-1..3)`, `@include text(small | medium)`
<!-- @end -->

<!-- @project-config:colors-tokens -->
**`src/sass/partials/config/_colors.scss`**

- Palette: `$blue`
- Semantic: `$primary-color`, `$secondary-color`, `$success-color`, `$info-color`, `$warning-color`
- Typography: `$body-color`, `$heading-color`, `$link-color`, `$link-hover-color`
<!-- @end -->

<!-- @project-config:variables-tokens -->
**`src/sass/partials/config/_variables.scss`**

- Container: `$container-padding-x`
- Menu: `$menu-breakpoint`
- Easing: `$custom-ease`
- Z-index: `$z-search`, `$z-header`, `$z-mobile-menu`, `$z-backdrop`, `$z-popup`
- Selection: `$text-selection-bg-color`, `$text-selection-color`
- Scrollbar: `$custom-scrollbar-enable`, `$track-inline-size`, `$track-background`, `$track-border-radius`, `$track-margin-block-start`, `$track-margin-block-end`, `$thumb-background`, `$thumb-border-radius`
<!-- @end -->

```scss
// ❌ NEVER — raw color, raw font family
.card {
	color: #080c11;
	font-family: 'Libre Baskerville', serif;
}
// ✅ ALWAYS — token for color/family; explicit inline values for type metrics (see §Typography)
.card {
	color: $body-color;
	font-family: $heading-font-family;
	font-size: fluid(24, 40);
	line-height: 1.2;
}
```

Always `@use` the partial in the consuming file rather than redeclaring values.

### When to add a new token vs. inline

Add to `_colors.scss` / `_typography.scss` / `_variables.scss` if:

- The value appears in **≥2 blocks** (or clearly will), OR
- It's a **brand or semantic concept** (brand color, section spacing step, z-index layer, easing curve), OR
- It's a value you keep re-deriving (a specific radius, a recurring border color)

Inline the raw value if it's a **one-off decorative value specific to this block** (e.g. a unique overlay opacity, a block-specific aspect ratio). When in doubt, prefer adding a token — hardcoded values drift faster than shared ones.

## Typography

**Write `font-size`, `line-height`, and `font-family` inline on each component**, scaled with `fluid()` where the design calls for it. Don't reach for the `@include font(...)` / `@include text(...)` mixins, and don't apply `.h1`–`.h6` utility classes to elements — both abstractions hide the actual type metrics and make it harder to match a specific design without touching the global tokens.

```scss
// ❌ NEVER — mixin / utility class hides the actual values
.title {
	@include font(h2);
}
<h2 class="h2">Title</h2>

// ✅ ALWAYS — values explicit at the component
.title {
	font-family: $heading-font-family;
	font-size: fluid(28, 48);
	line-height: 1.1;
}
<h2 class="title">Title</h2>
```

`$heading-font-family`, `$sans-serif`, etc. (the font-family vars) are still good — those are brand identity, not per-component type metrics. Size and leading stay inline.

Color and other tokens (`$body-color`, `$primary-color`, spacing/z-index/easing variables) keep using token reuse per §Config tokens.

## Logical Properties

Default to logical properties — they read correctly in both LTR and RTL and match the project's existing patterns.

| Use | Not |
| --- | --- |
| `margin-inline` / `margin-block` | `margin-left` / `margin-top` |
| `padding-inline` / `padding-block` | `padding-left` / `padding-top` |
| `inline-size` / `block-size` | `width` / `height` (when meaningful — pure pixel boxes can stay physical) |
| `inset-inline-start` / `inset-block-start` | `left` / `top` |
| `border-inline-start` | `border-left` |

Physical properties are fine when the value is genuinely physical (an icon's `width: 16px`, a divider's `border-bottom`), but for flow-aware layout (gutters, padding, content offsets) reach for logical.

## Vertical Stacks — don't reach for flex + gap

For a simple vertical stack (header + paragraph + button, FAQ items, etc.), use `display: block` with `margin-block` between siblings (or `> * + *` if you need a flow utility). Don't switch to `display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: …` just to get spacing — flex on a stack adds layout complexity (intrinsic shrink, baseline, alignment) you don't need and makes the container behave differently inside grids.

Reserve flex on a vertical stack for genuine cross-axis needs: centering children horizontally, anchoring one child to the bottom (`margin-block-start: auto`), or aligning items along the inline axis.

```scss
// ❌ NEVER — flex used purely for spacing
.stack {
	display: flex;
	flex-direction: column;
	gap: fluid(16, 24);
}

// ✅ ALWAYS — block flow with margin-block
.stack > * + * {
	margin-block-start: fluid(16, 24);
}
```
